@subsection The Core of the Evaluator

The evaluation process can be described as the interplay between two
procedures: @code{gEval} and @code{gApply}.

@subsubheading Eval

@code{gEval} takes as arguments an expression and an environment.  It classifies
the expression and directs its evaluation.  @code{gEval} is structured as a case
analysis of the syntactic type of the expression to be evaluated.  In this
edition the syntactic type is literal: admission has already parsed and
type-checked the program text before any effect, and the translation hands
@code{gEval} one @code{GExpr} value whose type @emph{is} the expression type, so
the case analysis is a @code{when} with one arm per node, and the compiler
counts the arms.  This @newterm{abstract syntax} is what makes it easy to see
how we can change the syntax of the language: a new form extends the sealed
family with a new node, and a derived form is rewritten in guest code into the
forms the evaluator already has --- never by bolting on a second parser.
@code{gEval} never sees surface text at all.

@noindent
@b{Primitive expressions}

@itemize @bullet

@item
For self-evaluating expressions --- the numeric and Boolean literals of the
core language --- @code{gEval} returns the value the literal denotes: a
@code{GNum} node answers its @code{GNumV}, a @code{GBool} node its
@code{GBoolV}.

@item
@code{gEval} must look up variables in the environment to find their values.

@end itemize

@noindent
@b{Special forms}

@itemize @bullet

@item
For data forms there is nothing to quote: a literal or a constructor call is
already the data it denotes, and an explicit syntax-tree value such as
@code{GApp(GVar("f"), GNum(1L))} is ordinary program data, because the sealed
family is a family of data classes (@ref{4.1.5}).

@item
An assignment to (or a definition of) a variable must recursively call
@code{gEval} to compute the new value to be associated with the variable.  The
environment must be modified to change (or create) the binding of the variable:
a @code{GSet} node rebinds a mutable cell, while @code{GLet} and
@code{GLetRec} introduce the binding their initializer computed.

@item
An @code{if} expression requires special processing of its parts, so as to
evaluate the consequent if the predicate is true, and otherwise to evaluate the
alternative.  The predicate must denote a Boolean: the kernel's @code{GIf}
continues with a @code{GBoolV} and answers nothing for any other value, which
is the kernel-side form of the edition's Boolean-only condition rule.

@item
A @code{lambda} expression must be transformed into an applicable procedure by
packaging together the parameters and body it specifies with the environment of
the evaluation: a @code{GLam} node produces a @code{GClosV}.

@item
A sequence of expressions must be evaluated in the order in which they appear:
@code{GBlock} runs its @code{GStmt} statements in order and answers the value
of the final expression.

@item
A case analysis must evaluate exactly the branch its subject selects:
@code{GWhen} carries the optional subject, its @code{GCase} branches, and the
fallback.  Reducing a case analysis to a nest of @code{GIf} tests in guest code
is the derived-form exercise of @ref{4.1.2}.

@end itemize

@noindent
@b{Combinations}

@itemize @bullet

@item
For a procedure application, @code{gEval} must recursively evaluate the operator
part and the operands of the combination.  The resulting procedure and
arguments are passed to @code{gApply}, which handles the actual procedure
application.

@end itemize

@noindent
Here is the definition of @code{gEval}.  The case analysis is the @code{when}
over the sealed expression family introduced in @ref{4.1.2}; every arm either
answers a value directly or recurs through @code{gEval}, so evaluation is
ordinary recursion --- the tail-call question the book answers with an explicit
dispatch loop is answered here instead by the machine of @ref{5.4.2}, whose
tail calls run in constant stack.  Errors are values, not exceptions: an arm
answers @code{null} for an unbound name, a premature read, or an ill-shaped
operand.  The @code{null} convention belongs to one plane only: the runtime
outcomes of the @emph{represented} program the kernel interprets.  Ill-typed
guest @emph{source} lives on the other plane and never reaches the kernel ---
admission rejects it statically, before any guest effect.  Within its plane the
@code{?: return null} chain stops the evaluation at the first failure:

@example kotlin
fun gEval(expr: GExpr, env: GFrame): GValue? =
    when (expr) @{
        is GNum -> GNumV(expr.n)

        is GBool -> GBoolV(expr.b)

        is GVar -> @{
            val found = env.lookup(expr.name)
            if (found == null || found is GUnassigned) @{
                null
            @} else @{
                found
            @}
        @}

        is GLam -> GClosV(expr.param, expr.body, env)

        is GApp -> @{
            val fn = gEval(expr.fn, env) ?: return null
            if (fn !is GClosV) @{
                null
            @} else @{
                val arg = gEval(expr.arg,
                    env) ?: return null
                gApply(fn, listOf(arg))
            @}
        @}

        is GLet -> @{
            val value = gEval(expr.init,
                env) ?: return null
            val frame = GFrame(mutableMapOf(expr.name to value),
                env)
            gEval(expr.body, frame)
        @}

        is GLetRec -> @{
            val frame = GFrame(mutableMapOf(expr.name to GUnassigned),
                env)
            val value = gEval(expr.init,
                frame) ?: return null
            frame.cells[expr.name] = value
            gEval(expr.body, frame)
        @}

        is GIf -> @{
            val test = gEval(expr.test,
                env) ?: return null
            if (test !is GBoolV) @{
                null
            @} else if (test.b) @{
                gEval(expr.onTrue, env)
            @} else @{
                gEval(expr.onFalse, env)
            @}
        @}

        is GAdd -> @{
            val left = gEval(expr.left,
                env) ?: return null
            val right = gEval(expr.right,
                env) ?: return null
            if (left is GNumV && right is GNumV) @{
                GNumV(left.n + right.n)
            @} else @{
                null
            @}
        @}

        is GMul -> @{
            val left = gEval(expr.left,
                env) ?: return null
            val right = gEval(expr.right,
                env) ?: return null
            if (left is GNumV && right is GNumV) @{
                GNumV(left.n * right.n)
            @} else @{
                null
            @}
        @}

        is GLt -> @{
            val left = gEval(expr.left,
                env) ?: return null
            val right = gEval(expr.right,
                env) ?: return null
            if (left is GNumV && right is GNumV) @{
                GBoolV(left.n < right.n)
            @} else @{
                null
            @}
        @}

        is GSet -> @{
            val value = gEval(expr.value,
                env) ?: return null
            if (env.assign(expr.name, value)) @{
                GBoolV(true)
            @} else @{
                null
            @}
        @}
    @}
@end example

@noindent
For clarity, @code{gEval}'s chain has been implemented as a case analysis using
a @code{when}.  The disadvantage of this is that our evaluator handles only the
forms named by the sealed family, and no new one can be evaluated without adding a
variant and its arm.  In many evaluators, dispatching on expression type
uses a data-directed style; @ref{Exercise 4.3} builds that alternative as a
@code{Map} of handlers keyed by node.
The book's ``unknown expression type'' fault cannot fire here:
every admitted form maps onto a typed node, so a malformed form fails at admission
with the typed error of the edition's @newterm{typed errors} --- before any
guest effect, as every ill-typed guest program does.

@subsubheading Apply

@code{gApply} takes two arguments, a procedure value and the list of arguments
to which the procedure should be applied.  It classifies procedures into two
kinds: a primitive applies through the runtime (we show that arm in
@ref{4.1.4}, where the primitive table of the language lives); it applies
compound procedures by evaluating the body of the procedure.  The environment
for the evaluation of the body of a compound procedure is constructed by
extending the base environment carried by the procedure to include a frame
that binds the parameter of the procedure to the argument to which the
procedure is to be applied.  The application arm of @code{gEval} feeds it:

@example kotlin
fun gApply(fn: GValue, args: List<GValue>): GValue? @{
    if (fn !is GClosV) return null
    val frame = GFrame(mutableMapOf(fn.param to args.get(0)),
        fn.env)
    val result = gEval(fn.body, frame) ?: return null
    if (result is GReturnV) return result.value
    if (result is GBreakV) return null
    if (result is GContinueV) return null
    return result
@}
@end example

@noindent
A closure answers its body's value; any other value answers @code{null}, the
kernel's not-applicable fault.  The @code{GApp} arm guards the operator before
it evaluates the operand, so a represented call whose operator is not a
procedure answers @code{null} without running the argument's effects --- the
evaluation order the witness kernel pins.  Because a multi-parameter lambda desugars to
nested one-parameter @code{GLam} closures, and a multi-argument call to nested
@code{GApp} applications (@ref{4.1.2}), one call to @code{gApply} binds one
parameter at a time and a curried application unwraps one closure per
argument.  A body with several statements is a @code{GBlock}; its statements
run in order and the block answers its final expression.

@subsubheading Procedure arguments

When @code{gEval} processes a procedure application, the operator is evaluated
before the operand --- and in the binary forms, the left operand before the
right --- because the arm's own calls run in the order written.  That is the
language's fixed order: the host evaluates left to right, and the kernel
inherits the order of its statements.  The order becomes a property of the
evaluator itself only when the evaluator's own recursion decides it.  The book
isolates that decision in one procedure, @code{listOfValues}: it takes as an
argument the operands of a combination, evaluates each in turn, and returns a
list of the corresponding values:@footnote{The book's original
@code{list-of-values} was an explicit recursion, written (rather than using
@code{map}) to emphasize the fact that the evaluator can be implemented
without any use of higher-order procedures (and thus could be written in a
language that doesn't have higher-order procedures), even though the language
that it supports will include higher-order procedures.  In this edition the
recursion visits the operands one at a time and stops at the first one whose
evaluation fails; the order it visits them in is exactly the choice that
exercise 4.1 makes observable.}

@example kotlin
fun listOfValues(operands: List<GExpr>,
    env: GFrame): List<GValue>? @{
    if (operands.isEmpty()) return emptyList()
    val first = gEval(operands.get(0),
        env) ?: return null
    val rest = listOfValues(operands.drop(1),
        env) ?: return null
    return listOf(first).plus(rest)
@}
@end example

@noindent
The kernel's unary @code{GApp} arm needs no such helper --- its order is fixed
by the two calls in the arm --- so the recursion above stands as the direct
formulation for any form that carries a whole operand list, such as the
argument list of a @code{GConstruct} node in the extended family
(@ref{4.1.2}), or the operand vector the primitive application of
@ref{4.1.4} reconstructs from a nested-@code{GApp} spine; in the syntax a
multi-argument call never carries such a list, because it desugars to nested
@code{GApp}.  It is exactly the recursion that
@ref{Exercise 4.1} rebuilds in both directions, to make the choice observable
rather than inherited.

@subsubheading Conditionals

The @code{GIf} arm evaluates the predicate part of an @code{if} expression in
the given environment.  If the result is true, @code{gEval} continues with the
consequent, otherwise it continues with the alternative:

@example kotlin
is GIf -> @{
    val test = gEval(expr.test, env) ?: return null
    if (test !is GBoolV) @{
        null
    @} else if (test.b) @{
        gEval(expr.onTrue, env)
    @} else @{
        gEval(expr.onFalse, env)
    @}
@}
@end example

@noindent
The use of @code{isTrue} in @code{evalIf} highlights the issue of the
connection between an implemented language and an implementation language.  The
predicate is evaluated in the language being implemented and thus yields a
value in that language.  The interpreter predicate @code{isTrue} translates
that value into a value that can be tested by the @code{if} in the
implementation language: The metacircular representation of truth might not be
the same as that of the host, Kotlin.@footnote{Here the language being
implemented is the guest language and the implementation language is Kotlin.
Contemplation of the meaning of @code{isTrue} here yields expansion of
consciousness without the abuse of substance.}

@subsubheading Sequences

A sequence is evaluated in the order in which its expressions occur.  The value
returned is the value of the final expression.  In the core language a
sequence is a chain of @code{GLet} forms whose bound name no later form reads
--- the shape @code{sharedCellProgram} takes when it bumps the cell before
reading it --- and the extended family gives a sequence its own node,
@code{GBlock} over a list of @code{GStmt} statements (@ref{4.1.2}).  A
@code{GBlock} with no statements answers @code{GUnitV}, as does one ending in a
non-expression statement: the guest grammar types an empty block as unit, so
the old reader's ``empty sequence'' parse error has no counterpart here.  A
@code{GReturnStmt} evaluates its value and answers the marker
@code{GReturnV(value)}; statement sequencing and @code{GBlock} pass any marker
through unchanged instead of continuing, so the marker travels past any
intermediate @code{GBlock}s to the application boundary, where @code{gApply}
unwraps it and answers its value.  @code{GBreakV} and @code{GContinueV} are
consumed by @code{GWhileStmt} (@ref{4.1.2}); a marker that reaches
@code{gApply} without a consumer answers @code{null}, the kernel's error
convention.  Where a
sequence's final expression is a tail call, the
kernel's plain recursion grows with the call depth --- recursion depth is a
resource, not a contract --- and the machine of @ref{5.4.2} is what runs tail
calls in constant stack.

@subsubheading Assignments and definitions

The assignment arm handles assignments to variables.  It calls @code{gEval}
to find the value to be assigned and transmits the variable and the resulting
value to the frame's @code{assign} to be installed in the designated
environment:

@example kotlin
is GSet -> @{
    val value = gEval(expr.value, env) ?: return null
    if (env.assign(expr.name, value)) @{
        GBoolV(true)
    @} else @{
        null
    @}
@}
@end example

@noindent
Definitions of variables are handled in a similar manner, as the binding forms
@code{GLet} and @code{GLetRec}: @code{GLet} installs the initializer's value in
a frame that extends the current one, and @code{GLetRec} --- the form for a
definition that refers to itself --- opens the frame with the name bound to
@code{GUnassigned}, evaluates the initializer in that frame, and patches in the
computed value before the body runs.@footnote{This tie-the-knot discipline
handles self-recursion, although a subtler issue remains in the handling of
internal definitions.  We will see what the problem is and how to solve it in
@ref{4.1.6}.}

@example kotlin
is GLetRec -> @{
    val frame = GFrame(mutableMapOf(expr.name to GUnassigned),
        env)
    val value = gEval(expr.init, frame) ?: return null
    frame.cells[expr.name] = value
    gEval(expr.body, frame)
@}
@end example

@noindent
We have chosen here to answer @code{GBoolV(true)} as the value of an
assignment.@footnote{As we said when we introduced definitions and
assignments, these values are implementation-dependent --- the implementor can
choose what value to return.  In the surface guest language an assignment or a
declaration is a statement and yields no observable value at all, so the
kernel's choice is visible only to represented programs that consume it.}

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 4.1}Exercise 4.1:} This host settles the question the
original leaves open: Kotlin always evaluates a procedure's operands from left
to right, so the metacircular evaluator inherits a fixed order, and we can
already tell which one --- there is nothing to observe.  The order becomes a
property of the evaluator itself only when the evaluator's own recursion
decides it: the explicit recursion of @code{listOfValues} is what stops the
host from deciding for us.  Write a version of @code{listOfValues} that
evaluates operands from left to right by construction, and a version that
evaluates them from right to left, and run the same operand-recording program
under both to see exactly what the host order had been deciding for us.
@end quotation

@node	4.1.2, 4.1.3, 4.1.1, 4.1
@subsection Representing Expressions

The evaluator is reminiscent of the symbolic differentiation program discussed
in @ref{2.3.2}.  Both programs operate on symbolic expressions.  In
both programs, the result of operating on a compound expression is determined
by operating recursively on the pieces of the expression and combining the
results in a way that depends on the type of the expression.  In both programs
we used data abstraction to decouple the general rules of operation from the
details of how expressions are represented.  In the differentiation program
this meant that the same differentiation procedure could deal with algebraic
expressions in prefix form, in infix form, or in some other form.  For the
evaluator, this means that the syntax of the language being evaluated is
determined solely by the parser that classifies each datum and carries its
pieces in a typed node.

Here is the specification of the syntax of our language --- the sealed family
of nodes the kernel evaluates, grown one form at a time from the core of
@ref{4.1.1}:

@example kotlin
sealed interface GExpr

// literals
data class GNum(val n: Long) : GExpr
data class GStr(val s: String) : GExpr
data class GDouble(val d: Double) : GExpr
data class GBool(val b: Boolean) : GExpr
data object GNull : GExpr

// names, bindings, and mutation
data class GVar(val name: String) : GExpr
data class GLam(val param: String,
    val body: GExpr) : GExpr
data class GApp(val fn: GExpr, val arg: GExpr) : GExpr
data class GLet(val name: String, val value: GExpr,
    val body: GExpr) : GExpr
data class GLetRec(val name: String, val value: GExpr,
    val body: GExpr) : GExpr
data class GSet(val name: String,
    val value: GExpr) : GExpr

// conditionals and type tests
data class GIf(val test: GExpr, val onTrue: GExpr,
    val onFalse: GExpr) : GExpr
data class GWhen(
    val subject: GExpr?,
    val cases: List<GCase>,
    val otherwise: GExpr?,
) : GExpr
data class GCase(val pattern: GExpr?, val body: GExpr)
data class GIs(val value: GExpr,
    val typeName: String) : GExpr

// primitive operations
data class GAdd(val left: GExpr,
    val right: GExpr) : GExpr
data class GSub(val left: GExpr,
    val right: GExpr) : GExpr
data class GMul(val left: GExpr,
    val right: GExpr) : GExpr
data class GDiv(val left: GExpr,
    val right: GExpr) : GExpr
data class GMod(val left: GExpr,
    val right: GExpr) : GExpr
data class GLt(val left: GExpr,
    val right: GExpr) : GExpr
data class GEq(val left: GExpr,
    val right: GExpr) : GExpr

// structured data and access
data class GConstruct(val className: String,
    val args: List<GExpr>) : GExpr
data class GMember(val target: GExpr,
    val name: String) : GExpr
data class GIndex(val target: GExpr,
    val index: GExpr) : GExpr

// statements and blocks
data class GBlock(val statements: List<GStmt>) : GExpr

sealed interface GStmt
data class GExprStmt(val expression: GExpr) : GStmt
data class GVarStmt(val name: String,
    val init: GExpr) : GStmt
data class GAssignStmt(val name: String,
    val value: GExpr) : GStmt
data class GWhileStmt(val cond: GExpr,
    val body: GBlock) : GStmt
data class GReturnStmt(val value: GExpr) : GStmt
data object GBreakStmt : GStmt
data object GContinueStmt : GStmt
@end example

@noindent
Every form of the object language is one of these nodes, and every node names
its pieces as typed fields --- so a malformed form is unrepresentable, and the
``unknown expression type'' fault of the book has nothing to catch.  The
object language of this evaluator is the guest language itself: a program
reaches the evaluator as these values after admission has parsed and
type-checked its source.

@itemize

@item
The only self-evaluating items are literals --- numbers, strings, doubles,
Booleans, and the null value.  The kernel answers the value each literal
denotes: a @code{GNum} node answers its @code{GNumV}, a @code{GBool} node its
@code{GBoolV}, and so on down the literal family.

@item
Variables are represented by names:

@example kotlin
// the variable node: GVar carries the name to look up
data class GVar(val name: String) : GExpr
@end example

@item
Data need no quotation: a literal or a constructor call is already the datum
it denotes, and an explicit syntax-tree value such as
@code{GApp(GVar("f"), GNum(1L))} is ordinary program data, because the sealed
family is a family of data classes (@ref{4.1.5}).@footnote{In the original the
reader sees a quoted expression as a quotation even when it is typed with the
quotation mark: @code{'a} is read as the quotation of the symbol
@code{a}.  As in @ref{2.3.1}, where data as values was introduced, the datum
here is simply a value built by constructors.  See
@ref{Exercise 2.55}.}

@item
Assignments rebind a mutable cell; the node carries the name and the value
expression:

@example kotlin
// the assignment node: GSet carries
// the name and the value expression
data class GSet(val name: String,
    val value: GExpr) : GExpr
@end example

@item
Definitions introduce bindings.  The declarations of the guest language
translate onto the two binding nodes: @code{GLet} for a plain binding, and
@code{GLetRec} for a binding whose initializer refers to the name being
bound.  A named function is syntactic sugar for a recursive binding of a
lambda:

@example kotlin
fun square(x: Long): Long = x * x
// the translation builds the same node as
// GLetRec("square", GLam("x", GMul(GVar("x"),
// GVar("x"))), <body>)
@end example

@noindent
The translation builds that node when it expands the function-definition
sugar, which is what the book's @code{make-lambda} constructor did.

@item
Conditionals test a predicate and continue with the consequent or the
alternative:@footnote{The value of an @code{if} expression when the predicate
is false and there is no alternative is unspecified in the original; the guest
grammar settles the question in its type rules rather than leaving it to the
implementor: an @code{if} with no alternative has type unit and answers
@code{GUnitV}.  We will support the use of @code{true} and @code{false} as
literals --- @code{GBool} nodes --- rather than as variables bound in the
global environment.  See @ref{4.1.4}.}

@example kotlin
data class GIf(val test: GExpr, val onTrue: GExpr,
    val onFalse: GExpr) : GExpr
@end example

@item
A block packages a sequence of statements into a single expression.  The node
carries the actual sequence, and ordinary list operations give the first
statement and the rest.@footnote{These selectors for a list of
statements --- and the corresponding ones for a list of operands --- are not
intended as a data abstraction.  They are introduced as mnemonic names for the
basic list operations in order to make it easier to understand the
explicit-control evaluator in @ref{5.4}.}

@example kotlin
// the block node: GBlock carries the statements in
// order
data class GBlock(val statements: List<GStmt>) : GExpr
@end example

@item
A procedure application applies an operator to its operand.  Every other
compound form of the language reaches the evaluator through one of the nodes
above or through an application:

@example kotlin
// the application node: GApp carries the operator and
// the operand
data class GApp(val fn: GExpr, val arg: GExpr) : GExpr
@end example

@end itemize

@subsubheading Derived expressions

Some special forms in our language can be defined in terms of expressions
involving other special forms, rather than being implemented directly.  One
example is a case analysis, which can be implemented as a nest of @code{GIf}
expressions.  For example, we can reduce the problem of evaluating the
expression

@example kotlin
when @{
    x > 0L -> x
    x == 0L -> @{ println("zero"); 0L @}
    else -> -x
@}
@end example

@noindent
to the problem of evaluating the following expression involving @code{GIf}
expressions and a block:

@example kotlin
if (x > 0L) @{
    x
@} else if (x == 0L) @{
    println("zero")
    0L
@} else @{
    -x
@}
@end example

@noindent
Implementing the evaluation of a case analysis in this way simplifies the
evaluator because it reduces the number of special forms for which the
evaluation process must be explicitly specified.

The translation carries the clauses of a @code{when} expression as typed
@code{GCase} values, and the function @code{whenToIfChain} transforms case
analyses into @code{GIf} nests.  A case analysis may carry a subject, and each
clause is a pattern to match against it, or --- in a subject-less
@code{when} --- a guard expression.  A subject pattern is either a type test
(a @code{GIs} node, which is how sealed dispatch selects a variant) or a value
the subject must equal; the rewrite honors that difference.  A clause is an
@code{else} clause when the case analysis carries its fallback in
@code{otherwise}.@footnote{The original conditional form leaves the
no-matching-case result unspecified.  This edition's guest grammar settles
the question in its type rules: a @code{GWhen} over a sealed subject must be
exhaustive, and a subjectless conditional dispatch must include a fallback.
Adding a sealed variant makes an incomplete dispatch a compile-time error.}

@example kotlin
fun whenToIfChain(expr: GWhen): GExpr @{
    val cases = expr.cases
    val subject = expr.subject
    if (cases.isEmpty()) return expr.otherwise ?: GBool(false)
    val first = cases.get(0)
    val rest = cases.drop(1)
    val pattern = first.pattern
    val test = when @{
        pattern == null -> GBool(true)
        pattern is GIs &&
            subject != null -> GIs(subject,
            pattern.typeName)
        subject != null -> GEq(subject, pattern)
        else -> pattern
    @}
    val alternative =
        if (rest.isEmpty()) expr.otherwise ?: GBool(false)
        else whenToIfChain(GWhen(subject,
                rest,
            expr.otherwise))
    return GIf(test, first.body, alternative)
@}
@end example

@noindent
Some guest forms, such as the @code{GWhen} node, can be implemented by syntax
transformations; these are called @newterm{derived expressions}.  The
@code{GLet} binding node is also derived (see @ref{Exercise 4.6}).@footnote{Some
language systems provide a mechanism that allows a user to add derived
expressions as syntax transformations without
modifying the evaluator.  Such a user-defined transformation is called a
@newterm{macro}.  Although it is easy to add an elementary mechanism for
defining macros, the resulting language has subtle name-conflict problems.
There has been much research on mechanisms for macro definition that do not
cause these difficulties.  See, for example, @ref{Kohlbecker 1986},
@ref{Clinger and Rees 1991}, and @ref{Hanson 1991}.}

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 4.2}Exercise 4.2:} Louis Reasoner plans to reorder the
arms of @code{gEval}'s @code{when} so that the arm for procedure
applications appears before the arm for assignments.  He argues that this
will make the interpreter more efficient: Since programs usually contain more
applications than assignments, definitions, and so on, his modified evaluator
will usually check fewer arms than the original before
identifying the type of an expression.

@enumerate a

@item
What is wrong with Louis's plan?  In the original untyped tree model, the
evaluator could mistake a binding form for a call with a familiar name.  Ask
the same question of this typed node family: what will his evaluator do with a
@code{GLet} node, which introduces a binding, and a @code{GSet} node, which
updates one?  What does this tell you about why the plan is harmless here,
or whether it still has a problem?

@item
Louis is upset that his plan didn't work.  He is willing to go to any lengths
to make his evaluator recognize procedure applications before it checks for
most other kinds of expressions.  Help him by changing the syntax of the
evaluated language so that procedure applications are explicitly marked ---
extend the sealed family with a marked application node and give the
translation the case that builds it --- so that an application can never be
mistaken for any other form, whatever order the arms are checked in.

@end enumerate
@end quotation

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 4.3}Exercise 4.3:} Rewrite @code{gEval} so that the
dispatch is done in data-directed style, using a table keyed by an
@emph{explicit kind tag} --- a tag field added to the sealed family, or a
hand-written @code{kindOf} function that answers one --- instead of one arm
