@example kotlin
fun secondOf(entry: Pair<String,
    Long>): Long = entry.second

fun findEntry(
    table: Map<String, Long>,
    key: String,
): Pair<String, Long>? @{
    if (table.containsKey(key)) @{
        return key to (table.get(key) ?: 0L)
    @}
    return null
@}

fun main() @{
    val table = mapOf("a" to 1L, "b" to 2L)
    val found = findEntry(table, "b")
    val answer: Long? =
        if (found == null) null else secondOf(found)
    if (answer == null) @{
        println(false)
    @} else @{
        println(answer)
    @}
@}
// => 2
@end example

@noindent
Modify the handling of the case analysis --- @code{whenToIfChain} and the
case-analysis nodes --- so that this computation is one clause with a
recipient: the test is the lookup, the recipient is @code{secondOf}, and the
fallback is @code{false}.  You may add the clause shape beside @code{GCase}
(for example a data class carrying the pattern and the recipient expression)
or teach the chain to apply a function-valued case body to the matched value;
either way the recipient is applied to the value the test produced, never to
the test expression itself.
@end quotation

@quotation
