@subsection Evaluator Data Structures

In addition to defining the external syntax of expressions, the evaluator
implementation must also define the data structures that the evaluator
manipulates internally, as part of the execution of a program, such as the
representation of procedures and environments and the representation of true
and false.

@subsubheading Testing of predicates

In the original, anything that is not the explicit false object counts as
true.  This edition has no truthiness: the guest grammar's Boolean-only
condition rule (the same rule its own @code{if} obeys) makes truth exactly
one value, and the kernel's @code{GIf} arm narrows on it:

@example kotlin
// the helper the GIf arm of 4.1.1 inlines: truth in the
// object language
// is the value GBoolV(true), which wraps --- but is not
// --- a Boolean
public fun isTrue(v: GValue): Boolean = v is GBoolV &&
    v.b
@end example

@noindent
The @code{GIf} arm of @ref{4.1.1} inlines exactly this test: it narrows the
predicate's value to @code{GBoolV} and reads its @code{b}.  The metacircular
representation of truth is therefore @emph{not} the host's truth: the object
language's true is the guest value @code{GBoolV(true)}, and the host only
meets it as the Kotlin @code{Boolean} that @code{isTrue} extracts.  A
represented program whose condition holds any other value --- a number, a
closure, @code{GUnitV} --- answers @code{null} rather than choosing a branch.

@subsubheading Representing procedures

To handle primitives, we assume that we have available the primitive table
described in @ref{4.1.4}:

@itemize @bullet

@item
the entry @code{defaultPrimitives.get(@var{name})}

@noindent
is the primitive procedure itself: a function that takes the list of
argument values and returns the result of the application.

@item
the test that the name misses the frame chain,
@code{env.lookup(@var{name}) == null}

@noindent
settles whether the name falls through to the table at all --- an
environment binding wins over any primitive of the same spelling
(@ref{3.4}).

@end itemize

@noindent
These mechanisms for handling primitives are further described in
@ref{4.1.4}.

Compound procedures are constructed from a parameter, a body, and an
environment using the constructor @code{GClosV}:

@example kotlin
data class GClosV(val param: String, val body: GExpr,
    val env: GFrame) : GValue
@end example

@noindent
The value is a @code{GClosV}: the parameter name, the body expression, and
the environment the lambda captured.  The printer keeps the environment part
out of sight, the way the book's @code{user-print} does (see
@ref{4.1.4}): a procedure prints by its own rendering function, never by
dumping the captured @code{GFrame} --- which may be a very large structure,
or may even contain cycles.

@subsubheading Operations on Environments

The evaluator needs operations for manipulating environments.  As explained in
@ref{3.2}, an environment is a sequence of frames, where each frame is
a table of bindings that associate variables with their corresponding values.
We use the following operations for manipulating environments:

@itemize @bullet

@item
@code{lookup(⟨@var{env}⟩, ⟨@var{name}⟩)}

returns the value that is bound to the name @code{⟨}@var{name}@code{⟩} in the environment
@code{⟨}@var{env}@code{⟩}, or signals an error if the variable is unbound.

@item
@code{extend(⟨@var{names}⟩, ⟨@var{values}⟩, ⟨@var{base-env}⟩)}

returns a new environment, consisting of a new frame in which the symbols in
the list @code{⟨}@var{names}@code{⟩} are bound to the corresponding elements in the list
@code{⟨}@var{values}@code{⟩}, where the enclosing environment is the environment
@code{⟨}@var{base-env}@code{⟩}.

@item
@code{define(⟨@var{env}⟩, ⟨@var{name}⟩, ⟨@var{value}⟩)}

adds to the first frame in the environment @code{⟨}@var{env}@code{⟩} a new binding that
associates the variable @code{⟨}@var{name}@code{⟩} with the value @code{⟨}@var{value}@code{⟩}.

@item
@code{set(⟨@var{env}⟩, ⟨@var{name}⟩, ⟨@var{value}⟩)}

changes the binding of the variable @code{⟨}@var{name}@code{⟩} in the environment @code{⟨}@var{env}@code{⟩}
so that the variable is now bound to the value @code{⟨}@var{value}@code{⟩}, or signals an
error if the variable is unbound.

@end itemize

@noindent
To implement these operations we use the environments of @ref{3.2}: an
environment is a chain of frames, each frame a name-to-value table, and the
enclosing environment of an environment is the frame's parent.  There is no
empty-environment sentinel: the chain simply ends at the global frame, where
the parent is absent.  The four operations are thin members of @code{GFrame},
each a single step over that representation.

To extend an environment by a new frame that associates variables with values,
we make a frame binding each name to the value at the same position, and we
adjoin this to the environment.  Arity mismatches never reach this code in the
core: a multi-argument call desugars to nested one-argument applications
(@ref{4.1.2}), so exactly one name meets exactly one value per binding step.

@example kotlin
val frame = GFrame(mutableMapOf(name to value), env)
@end example

@noindent
To look up a variable in an environment, we search the table of the first
frame.  If we find the desired variable, we return its value.  If we do not
find the variable in the current frame, we search the enclosing environment,
and so on.  If the chain runs out, the lookup answers @code{null} --- the
kernel's unbound-name convention on its error plane (@ref{4.1.1}).

@example kotlin
fun lookup(name: String): GValue? @{
    var here: GFrame? = this
    while (here != null) @{
        val hit = here.cells[name]
        if (hit != null) @{
            return hit
        @}
        here = here.parent
    @}
    return null
@}
@end example

@noindent
To set a variable to a new value in a specified environment, we search for the
variable, just as in @code{lookup}, and change the corresponding value when we
find it.  If the chain runs out, the assignment answers false.

@example kotlin
fun assign(name: String, value: GValue): Boolean @{
    var here: GFrame? = this
    while (here != null) @{
        if (here.cells.containsKey(name)) @{
            here.cells[name] = value
            return true
        @}
        here = here.parent
    @}
    return false
@}
@end example

@noindent
To define a variable, we add the binding to the first frame's table, shadowing
any outer binding of the same name without touching it --- which is what the
@code{GLet} arm of @code{gEval} does when it builds
@code{GFrame(mutableMapOf(name to value), env)}.

@noindent
Each frame is a mutable map behind a @code{val}, the frame model of the
re-cut @ref{3.2}: defining --- the @code{GLet} arm --- allocates a child
frame whose map holds the new binding, so an inner name shadows an outer one
without touching it, while assignment rewrites the entry of the frame that
owns the name and is visible through every closure sharing it
(@ref{3.4}).  The kernel's @code{lookup} and @code{assign} walk the parent
chain; everything else about the representation is deliberately invisible to
the evaluator.@footnote{Frames are not really a data abstraction in the
following code: @code{assign} and the @code{GLet} arm write the frame's map
directly.  The purpose of the @code{GFrame} operations is to make the
environment-manipulation parts easy to read.}

The method described here is only one of many plausible ways to represent
environments.  Since we used data abstraction to isolate the rest of the
evaluator from the detailed choice of representation, we could change the
environment representation if we wanted to.  (See @ref{Exercise 4.11}.)  A
production evaluator must consider lookup speed, especially in deeply nested
scopes.  This representation is conceptually simple but inefficient for deep
frame chains and would not ordinarily be used in production.@footnote{The
drawback of this representation (and the variant in @ref{Exercise 4.11}) is
that the evaluator may have to search
through many frames in order to find the binding for a given variable.  (Such
an approach is referred to as @newterm{deep binding}.)  One way to avoid this
inefficiency is to make use of a strategy called @newterm{lexical addressing},
which will be discussed in @ref{5.5.6}.}

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 4.11}Exercise 4.11:} Instead of the runtime's frame
(a name-to-value map), represent a frame as a list of bindings, where each
binding is a name-value pair.  Rewrite the environment operations to use this
alternative representation.
@end quotation

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 4.12}Exercise 4.12:} The operations
--- binding a name (@code{GLet}), @code{assign} and
@code{lookup} --- can be expressed in terms of more abstract
procedures for traversing the environment structure.  Define abstractions that
capture the common patterns and redefine the three operations in terms of these
abstractions.
@end quotation

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 4.13}Exercise 4.13:} The guest language allows us to
create new bindings for variables --- with declarations and the binding forms
of @ref{4.1.2} --- but provides no way to get rid of bindings.  Implement for
the evaluator a special form
@code{make-unbound!} that removes the binding of a given name from the
environment in which the @code{make-unbound!} expression is evaluated.  This
problem is not completely specified.  For example, should we remove only the
binding in the first frame of the environment?  Complete the specification and
justify any choices you make.
@end quotation

@node	4.1.4, 4.1.5, 4.1.3, 4.1
@subsection Running the Evaluator as a Program

Given the evaluator, we have in our hands a description (expressed in Kotlin) of
the process by which the language's expressions are evaluated.  One advantage of
expressing the evaluator as a program is that we can run the program.  This
gives us, running within Kotlin, a working model of how the language itself
evaluates expressions.  This can serve as a framework for experimenting with
evaluation rules, as we shall do later in this chapter.

Our evaluator program reduces expressions ultimately to the application of
primitive procedures.  Therefore, all that we need to run the evaluator is to
create a mechanism that calls on the underlying Kotlin runtime to model the
application of primitive procedures.

There must be a binding for each primitive procedure name, so that when
@code{gEval} evaluates the operator of an application of a primitive, it will
find something to apply.  We thus set up a table that associates the name of
each primitive with the Kotlin function that implements it.  The literals
@code{true} and @code{false} need no entries at all: they are @code{GBool}
nodes, and the object language has no variables spelled after them.

@example kotlin
// a primitive entry: its name, and the Kotlin function
// applied to
// the argument values
public val defaultPrimitives: Map<String,
    (List<GValue>) -> GValue?> =
    mapOf(
        "map" to ::mapPrimitive,
        // ⟨more primitives⟩
    )
@end example

@noindent
The table is consulted by name at the application step, and only after the
frame chain has had its say: a name bound in the environment wins over any
primitive of the same spelling, because an inner binding shadows an outer one
(@ref{3.4}).  Only a name that resolves nowhere in the chain falls through to
the table.  User-defined and primitive mapping sit side by side, which is
what @ref{Exercise 4.14} probes.@footnote{Any function written in Kotlin can be used as a primitive
for the metacircular evaluator.  The name of a primitive installed in the
evaluator need not be the same as the name of its implementation in Kotlin;
the names are the same here because the metacircular evaluator implements the
guest language itself.  Thus, for example, we could put an entry named
@code{first} for a head function, or an entry named @code{square} for a
squaring function, in the table of @code{defaultPrimitives}.}

@noindent
The extended application step consults that table first.  A call of a
primitive names it directly, while a multi-argument call has already desugared
to a spine of nested @code{GApp} nodes (@ref{4.1.2}); the step flattens the
spine back into one operand vector so a primitive can see all of its arguments
at once.  The vector is evaluated left to right by the @code{listOfValues}
recursion of @ref{4.1.1} --- exactly where the operand order of
@ref{Exercise 4.1} lives:

@example kotlin
fun head(expr: GExpr): GExpr = if (expr is GApp) head(expr.fn) else expr

fun operands(expr: GExpr): List<GExpr> =
    if (expr is GApp) operands(expr.fn).plus(expr.arg) else emptyList()

fun gApplyExpr(expr: GApp, env: GFrame): GValue? @{
    val name = head(expr)
    if (name is GVar && env.lookup(name.name) == null) @{
        val primitive = defaultPrimitives.get(name.name)
        if (primitive != null) @{
            val args = listOfValues(operands(expr),
                env) ?: return null
            return primitive(args)
        @}
    @}
    val fn = gEval(expr.fn, env) ?: return null
    if (fn !is GClosV) return null
    val arg = gEval(expr.arg, env) ?: return null
    return gApply(fn, listOf(arg))
@}
@end example

@noindent
The @code{GApp} arm of @code{gEval} delegates here.  Its fallback is the arm
of @ref{4.1.1} verbatim --- operator narrowed to @code{GClosV} before the
operand is evaluated --- so a name that is bound in the environment, or that
misses the table entirely, resolves through the frame chain exactly as a
user-defined procedure does.

@noindent
To apply a primitive procedure, we simply call the host implementation
function with the argument vector.  The source chapter required a helper
because its evaluator and host shared an application-function name; in Kotlin,
the host call is an ordinary function call.  The kernel's @code{gApply}
(@ref{4.1.1}) models guest application.  One twist remains: the guest
@code{map} must apply procedure values, which a plain primitive cannot do on
its own, so the @code{map} primitive re-enters @code{gApply} before its own
work runs, as shown below.

@example kotlin
fun mapApply(fn: GValue,
    items: List<GValue>): List<GValue>? @{
    if (items.isEmpty()) return emptyList()
    val first = gApply(fn,
        listOf(items.get(0))) ?: return null
    val rest = mapApply(fn,
        items.drop(1)) ?: return null
    return listOf(first).plus(rest)
@}

fun mapPrimitive(args: List<GValue>): GValue? @{
    val fn = args.get(0)
    val items = args.get(1)
    if (items !is GListV) return null
    return GListV(mapApply(fn,
        items.items) ?: return null)
@}
@end example

@noindent
@code{mapPrimitive} is the footnote's twist made concrete: @code{fn} is a
procedure value, so the primitive reaches for @code{gApply} --- the only
application mechanism the kernel has --- rather than calling @code{fn} as a
host function.  Without that re-entry the host's own @code{map} would not
know how to call it at all, which is the point of @ref{Exercise 4.14}.

For convenience in running the metacircular evaluator, the engine provides a
@newterm{driver loop} that models the read-eval-print loop of a system: it
reads the program text, admits it, evaluates it, and answers one result.  The
read step is where this edition differs most: @code{Admission.admit} parses
and type-checks the text @emph{before any effect}, so a malformed or
ill-typed program is rejected without one line of it running.@footnote{Admission is given code, like the book's @code{read}: it takes
the program text and answers either a typed diagnostic or a checked program.
For example, if the text is @code{println(23L + x)} and @code{x} is unbound,
admission answers an @code{UndeclaredName} diagnostic at the position of
@code{x} --- and the program never runs.}  An interleaved prompt would bury a
program's own output, so the driver records a bare transcript instead, under
the one-transcript contract: @code{RunResult.output} is exactly the ordered
@code{print}/@code{println} stream of the program, @code{RunResult.error} is
the first typed guest error if the program raised one at run time, and
@code{RunResult.mainValue} is the value of @code{main}'s completion.  The two
error planes stay apart: a rejected source answers its diagnostic before any
effect, while a run that traps carries its category in
@code{RunResult.error}.

@example kotlin
public fun runProgram(text: String): String =
    Direct.run(text, Mode.CORE).fold(
        @{ e -> "Error: " + e.category @},
        @{ run -> run.output @},
    )
@end example

@noindent
What the program prints is its own business --- and that is this edition's
form of the book's @code{user-print} concern.  The environment part of a
compound procedure, which may be a very large structure (or may even contain
cycles), cannot leak into a transcript: printing a structured value is
rejected outright, and a program that wants to show one renders it with its
own function returning @code{String}:

@example kotlin
runProgram(
    """
    fun square(x: Long): Long = x * x

    fun main() @{
        println(square(6L))
    @}
    """.trimIndent(),
)
// => 36
@end example

@noindent
Now all we need to do to run the evaluator is to hand the driver a program.
Here is a sample interaction, with the result list rendered by the program's
own @code{show}, since a list is a structured value:

@example kotlin
runProgram(
    """
    fun myAppend(xs: List<Long>,
        ys: List<Long>): List<Long> = xs.plus(ys)

    fun show(xs: List<Long>): String @{
        var out = ""
        var first = true
        for (x in xs) @{
            if (first) @{
                first = false
            @} else @{
                out = out + " "
            @}
            out = out + "$@{x@}"
        @}
        return out
    @}

    fun main() @{
        println(show(myAppend(listOf(1L,
                        2L,
                    3L),
                        listOf(4L,
                        5L,
            6L))))
    @}
    """.trimIndent(),
)
// => 1 2 3 4 5 6
@end example

@noindent
The definition prints nothing; the transcript's only line is the program's own
rendering of the result.

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 4.14}Exercise 4.14:} Eva Lu Ator and Louis Reasoner
are each experimenting with the metacircular evaluator.  Eva types in the
definition of @code{map}, and runs some test programs that use it.  They work
fine.  Louis, in contrast, has installed the system version of @code{map} as a
primitive for the metacircular evaluator.  When he tries it, things go terribly
wrong.  Explain why Louis's @code{map} fails even though Eva's works.
@end quotation

@node	4.1.5, 4.1.6, 4.1.4, 4.1
@subsection Data as Programs

In thinking about a Kotlin program that evaluates guest programs, an analogy
might be helpful.  One operational view of the meaning of a program is that a
program is a description of an abstract (perhaps infinitely large) machine.
For example, consider the familiar program to compute factorials:

@example kotlin
runProgram(
    """
    fun factorial(n: Long): Long = if (n == 1L) 1L else factorial(n -
        1L) * n

    fun main() @{
        println(factorial(5L))
    @}
    """.trimIndent(),
)
// => 120
@end example

@noindent
We may regard this program as the description of a machine containing parts
that decrement, multiply, and test for equality, together with a two-position
switch and another factorial machine. (The factorial machine is infinite
because it contains another factorial machine within it.)  @ref{Figure 4.2} is
a flow diagram for the factorial machine, showing how the parts are wired
together.

