@end quotation

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 4.18}Exercise 4.18:} Consider an alternative strategy
for scanning out definitions that translates the example in the text to

@example kotlin
GVarStmt("u", GNull)
// the names are created first
GVarStmt("v", GNull)
GVarStmt("a", ⟨@var{e1}⟩)      // a and b are fresh names the
GVarStmt("b", ⟨@var{e2}⟩)
// interpreter creates, and both
GAssignStmt("u", GVar("a"))
// initializers run before either
GAssignStmt("v", GVar("b"))
// of the user's names is assigned
⟨@var{e3}⟩
@end example

Here @code{a} and @code{b} are meant to represent new variable names, created
by the interpreter, that do not appear in the user's program.  Consider the
@code{solve} procedure of @ref{3.5.4} --- the section's opening sketch, whose
two bindings refer to each other, with the delayed integrand the section ties
back:

@example kotlin
fun solve(f: (Double) -> Double, y0: Double,
    dt: Double): LStream<Double> @{
    val y: LStream<Double> = integral(lazy @{ dy @},
        y0,
        dt)
    val dy: LStream<Double> = streamMap(f, y)
    return y
@}
@end example

