@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 4.21}Exercise 4.21:} Amazingly, Louis's intuition in
@ref{Exercise 4.20} is correct.  It is indeed possible to specify recursive
procedures without using @code{letrec} (or even a named definition), although
the method for accomplishing this is much more subtle than Louis imagined.
The following program computes 10 factorial by applying a recursive factorial
procedure---a procedure that receives itself as an argument:@footnote{This
example illustrates a programming trick for formulating recursive procedures
without using a definition.  The most general trick of this sort is the
@math{Y} @newterm{operator}, which can be used to give a ``pure
λ-calculus'' implementation of recursion.  See @ref{Stoy 1977} for details
on λ-calculus and @ref{Gabriel 1988} for an exposition of the @math{Y}
operator.}

@example kotlin
runProgram(
    """
    data class Rec(val body: (Rec, Long) -> Long)

    fun main() @{
        val fact = Rec(@{ self: Rec, k: Long ->
            if (k == 1L) 1L else k * self.body(self,
                k - 1L)
        @})
        println(fact.body(fact, 10L))
    @}
    """.trimIndent(),
)
// => 3628800
@end example

@noindent
The @code{Rec} wrapper only exists to give the trick a type: it holds one
property, @code{body}, a function that takes the procedure itself and the
argument.  The self-application is the call @code{self.body(self, k - 1L)} ---
the procedure hands itself to its own body, which is what lets the recursion
run with no named definition to refer to.

