@node	4.1.7, 4.2, Chapter 4, 4.1
@subsection Separating Syntactic Analysis from Execution

The evaluator implemented above is simple, but it is very inefficient, because
the syntactic analysis of expressions is interleaved with their execution.
Thus if a program is executed many times, its syntax is analyzed many times.
Consider, for example, evaluating @code{factorial(4L)} using the following
definition of @code{factorial}:

@example kotlin
runProgram(
    """
    fun factorial(n: Long): Long = if (n == 1L) 1L else factorial(n -
        1L) * n

    fun main() @{
        println(factorial(4L))
    @}
    """.trimIndent(),
)
// => 24
@end example

@noindent
Each time @code{factorial} is called, the evaluator must determine that the
body is an @code{if} expression and extract the predicate.  Only then can it
evaluate the predicate and dispatch on its value.  Each time it evaluates the
expression @code{factorial(n - 1L) * n}, or the subexpressions
@code{factorial(n - 1L)} and @code{n - 1L}, the evaluator must perform the
case analysis in @code{gEval} to determine that the expression is an
application, and must extract its operator and operands.  This analysis is
expensive.  Performing it repeatedly is wasteful.

We can transform the evaluator to be significantly more efficient by arranging
things so that syntactic analysis is performed only once.@footnote{This
technique is an integral part of the compilation process, which we shall
discuss in @ref{Chapter 5}.  The analysis-first technique appeared in
Jonathan Rees's interpreter for the T project (@ref{Rees and Adams 1982}).
Marc @ref{Feeley (1986)} (see also @ref{Feeley and Lapalme 1987})
independently developed it in his master's thesis.}
We split @code{gEval}, which takes an expression and an environment, into two
parts.  The procedure @code{analyze} takes only the
expression.  It performs the syntactic analysis and returns a new procedure,
the @newterm{execution procedure}, that encapsulates the work to be done in
executing the analyzed expression.  The execution procedure takes an
environment as its argument and completes the evaluation.  In this edition the
execution procedure is a Kotlin function value --- an environment-to-value
function over the @code{Raise<GuestError>} context through which the edition's
typed errors travel.  This saves work
because @code{analyze} will be called only once on an expression, while the
execution procedure may be called many times.

With the separation into analysis and execution, @code{gEval} now becomes

@example kotlin
// The execution procedure: the analyzed expression
// awaiting an
    // environment.
public typealias Exec = context(Raise<GuestError>)
(GFrame) -> GValue?

context(r: Raise<GuestError>)
public fun gEval(
    expr: GExpr,
    env: GFrame,
): GValue? = analyze(expr)(env)
@end example

@noindent
The result of calling @code{analyze} is the execution procedure to be applied
to the environment.  The @code{analyze} procedure is the same case analysis as
performed by the original @code{gEval} of @ref{4.1.1}, except that the
procedures to which we dispatch perform only analysis, not full evaluation:

@example kotlin
fun analyze(expr: GExpr): Exec =
    when (expr) @{
        is GNum -> @{
            analyzedSelfEvaluating(GNumV(expr.n))
        @}

        is GBool -> @{
            analyzedSelfEvaluating(GBoolV(expr.b))
        @}

        is GStr -> @{
            analyzedSelfEvaluating(GStrV(expr.s))
        @}

        is GDouble -> @{
            analyzedSelfEvaluating(GDoubleV(expr.d))
        @}

        is GNull -> @{
            analyzedSelfEvaluating(GNullV)
        @}

        is GVar -> @{
            analyzedVariable(expr.name)
        @}

        is GSet -> @{
            analyzedAssignment(expr.name,
                analyze(expr.value))
        @}

        // derived
        is GLet -> @{
            analyze(letToCombination(expr))
        @}

        is GLetRec -> @{
            analyzedRecursive(expr.name,
                analyze(expr.init),
                analyze(expr.body))
        @}

        is GIf -> @{
            analyzedIf(
                analyze(expr.test),
                analyze(expr.onTrue),
                analyze(expr.onFalse),
            )
        @}

        is GLam -> @{
            analyzedLambda(expr.param,
                analyze(expr.body))
        @}

        is GBlock -> @{
            analyzeSequence(expr.statements)
        @}

        // derived
        is GWhen -> @{
            analyze(whenToIfChain(expr))
        @}

        is GAdd -> @{
            analyzedBinary(expr.left,
                expr.right) @{ l,
                r ->
                if (l is GNumV &&
                    r is GNumV) GNumV(l.n + r.n) else null
            @}
        @}

        is GMul -> @{
            analyzedBinary(expr.left,
                expr.right) @{ l,
                r ->
                if (l is GNumV &&
                    r is GNumV) GNumV(l.n * r.n) else null
            @}
        @}

        is GLt -> @{
            analyzedBinary(expr.left,
                expr.right) @{ l,
                r ->
                if (l is GNumV &&
                    r is GNumV) GBoolV(l.n < r.n) else
                    null
            @}
        @}

        is GApp -> @{
            analyzedApplication(expr)
        @}
    @}
@end example

@noindent
The seam is the @code{when} itself: an extension adds the case for its forms
right there, and the case fires at every nesting depth because every analysis
recurses through @code{analyze}.  And as in @ref{4.1.1}, the book's ``unknown
expression type'' fault cannot fire: the typed nodes leave nothing
unrecognized.

Here is the simplest syntactic analysis procedure, which handles
self-evaluating expressions.  It returns an execution procedure that ignores
its environment argument and just returns the value:

@example kotlin
fun analyzedSelfEvaluating(v: GValue): Exec = @{ _ -> v @}
@end example

@noindent
The guest language has no quotation form --- its data objects are built
directly as @code{GExpr} values, as in @ref{4.1.5} --- so the efficiency gain
the original found in quoted expressions shows up in the literal arms above
instead: the node's field is read once, during analysis, and every later
execution of the procedure simply returns the value.  Looking up a variable
value must still be done in the execution phase, since this depends upon
knowing the environment.@footnote{There is, however, an
important part of the variable search that @emph{can} be done as part of the
syntactic analysis.  As we will show in @ref{5.5.6}, one can determine
the position in the environment structure where the value of the variable will
be found, thus obviating the need to scan the environment for the entry that
matches the variable.}

@example kotlin
fun analyzedVariable(name: String): Exec = @{ env ->
    val found = env.lookup(name)
    if (found == null || found is GUnassigned) null else found
@}
@end example

@noindent
@code{analyzedAssignment} also must defer actually setting the variable until
the execution, when the environment has been supplied.  However, the fact that
the assigned expression can be analyzed (recursively) during analysis is a major
gain in efficiency, because the assigned expression will now be analyzed only
once.  The same holds true for bindings.  The @code{GSet} and @code{GLetRec}
clauses of @code{analyze} pass the name and the already analyzed value
procedure:

@example kotlin
fun analyzedAssignment(
    name: String,
    vproc: Exec,
): Exec =
    @{ env ->
        val value = vproc(env)
        if (value == null) @{
            null
        @} else if (env.assign(name, value)) @{
            GBoolV(true)
        @} else @{
            null
        @}
    @}
@end example

@noindent
The recursive-binding clause opens the frame first --- the name bound to
@code{GUnassigned}, the allocate-first discipline of @ref{4.1.3} --- runs the
analyzed initializer in that frame, patches in the computed value, and only
then runs the analyzed body:

@example kotlin
fun analyzedRecursive(
    name: String,
    vproc: Exec,
    bproc: Exec,
): Exec =
    @{ env ->
        val frame = GFrame(mutableMapOf(name to GUnassigned),
            env)
        val value = vproc(frame)
        if (value == null) @{
            null
        @} else @{
            frame.cells[name] = value
            bproc(frame)
        @}
    @}
@end example

@noindent
For @code{if} expressions, we extract and analyze the predicate, consequent,
and alternative at analysis time.

@example kotlin
fun analyzedIf(
    pproc: Exec,
    cproc: Exec,
    aproc: Exec,
): Exec =
    @{ env ->
        val test = pproc(env)
        if (test == null) @{
            null
        @} else if (isTrue(test)) @{
            cproc(env)
        @} else @{
            aproc(env)
        @}
    @}
@end example

@noindent
Analyzing a @code{GLam} expression also achieves a major gain in efficiency:
We analyze the @code{GLam} body only once, even though procedures resulting
from evaluation of the @code{GLam} may be applied many times.  The analyzed
body is stored in a table keyed by the procedure object, at the
moment the execution procedure creates the procedure, so every later
application finds the body already analyzed (this is what @ref{Exercise 4.24}
measures):

@example kotlin
// The analyzed bodies, one per procedure object.
val bodies: MutableMap<GClosV, Exec> = mutableMapOf()

fun analyzedLambda(
    param: String,
    body: GExpr,
): Exec @{
    val bproc = analyze(body)
    return @{ env ->
        val procedure = GClosV(param, body, env)
        bodies[procedure] = bproc
        procedure
    @}
@}
@end example

@noindent
Analysis of a block's statements (as in the body of a @code{GLam} expression)
is more involved.@footnote{See @ref{Exercise 4.23}
for some insight into the processing of sequences.} Each statement in the
block is analyzed, yielding an execution procedure.  These execution
procedures are combined to produce an execution procedure that takes an
environment as argument and sequentially calls each individual execution
procedure with the environment as argument --- stopping as soon as one of them
fails or answers a control marker, which the block passes through unchanged,
just as the kernel's statement sequencing does:

@example kotlin
fun analyzeSequence(statements: List<GStmt>): Exec @{
    if (statements.isEmpty()) @{
        return @{ _ -> GUnitV @}
    @}
    val procs = statements.map @{ analyzeStatement(it) @}
    var combined: Exec = procs.last()
    for (i in procs.size - 2 downTo 0) @{
        val earlier = procs[i]
        val later = combined
        combined = @{ env ->
            val value = earlier(env)
            if (value == null || value is GReturnV ||
                value is GBreakV || value is GContinueV) @{
                value
            @} else @{
                later(env)
            @}
        @}
    @}
    return combined
@}
@end example

@noindent
The statement analyzer dispatches on the statement family the way the kernel's
statement runner does: an expression statement runs its expression, a
declaration binds its name in the current frame, an assignment reuses
@code{analyzedAssignment}, a @code{return} wraps the analyzed value in
@code{GReturnV}, and the two loop markers are the values they name.  The
@code{while} arm keeps the break/continue discipline at the loop, where the
kernel consumes those markers:

@example kotlin
fun analyzeStatement(stmt: GStmt): Exec =
    when (stmt) @{
        is GExprStmt -> analyze(stmt.expression)

        is GVarStmt -> @{
            val vproc = analyze(stmt.init)
            @{ env ->
                val value = vproc(env)
                if (value == null) @{
                    null
                @} else @{
                    env.cells[stmt.name] = value
                    GUnitV
                @}
            @}
        @}

        is GAssignStmt -> analyzedAssignment(stmt.name,
            analyze(stmt.value))

        is GWhileStmt -> analyzedWhile(stmt.cond,
            stmt.body)

        is GReturnStmt -> @{
            val vproc = analyze(stmt.value)
            @{ env ->
                val value = vproc(env)
                if (value == null) null else GReturnV(value)
            @}
        @}

        is GBreakStmt -> @{ _ -> GBreakV @}

        is GContinueStmt -> @{ _ -> GContinueV @}
    @}

fun analyzedWhile(cond: GExpr, body: GBlock): Exec @{
    val cproc = analyze(cond)
    val bproc = analyzeSequence(body.statements)
    return @{ env -> runWhile(cproc, bproc, env) @}
@}

fun runWhile(
    cproc: Exec,
    bproc: Exec,
    env: GFrame,
): GValue? @{
    while (true) @{
        val test = cproc(env) ?: return null
        if (test !is GBoolV) return null
        if (!test.b) return GUnitV
        val value = bproc(env) ?: return null
        if (value is GReturnV) return value
        if (value is GBreakV) return GUnitV
        // GContinueV, like an ordinary answer,
            // falls through to the re-test.
    @}
@}
@end example

@noindent
The arithmetic and comparison nodes are the same shape several times over:
both operands are analyzed once, and the execution procedure combines their
values with the operation @code{gEval} would have applied (@ref{4.1.1}):

@example kotlin
fun analyzedBinary(
    left: GExpr,
    right: GExpr,
    combine: (GValue, GValue) -> GValue?,
): Exec @{
    val lproc = analyze(left)
    val rproc = analyze(right)
    return @{ env ->
        val l = lproc(env)
        if (l == null) @{
            null
        @} else @{
            val r = rproc(env)
            if (r == null) null else combine(l, r)
        @}
    @}
@}
@end example

@noindent
To analyze an application, we analyze the operator and the operand and
construct an execution procedure that calls the operator execution procedure
(to obtain the actual procedure to be applied) and the operand execution
procedure (to obtain the actual argument).  We then pass these to
@code{executeApplication}, which is the analog of @code{gApply} in
@ref{4.1.1}.  @code{executeApplication} differs from @code{gApply} in that the
procedure body for a compound procedure has already been analyzed, so there is
no need to do further analysis.  Instead, we just call the stored execution procedure for
the body on the extended environment.  A name the frame chain does not answer
falls through to the primitive table, exactly as in @ref{4.1.4} --- and, as
there, an environment binding wins over any primitive of the same spelling:

@example kotlin
fun analyzedApplication(expr: GApp): Exec @{
    val name = head(expr)
    val aprocs = operands(expr).map @{ analyze(it) @}
    val fproc = analyze(expr.fn)
    val aproc = analyze(expr.arg)
    return @{ env ->
        val primitive =
            if (name is GVar &&
                env.lookup(name.name) == null)
                defaultPrimitives.get(name.name)
            else null
        if (primitive == null) @{
            val fn = fproc(env)
            if (fn !is GClosV) @{
                null
            @} else @{
                val arg = aproc(env)
                if (arg == null) null else
                    executeApplication(fn, listOf(arg))
            @}
        @} else @{
            runPrimitive(primitive, aprocs, env)
        @}
    @}
@}

fun runPrimitive(
    primitive: (List<GValue>) -> GValue?,
    aprocs: List<Exec>,
    env: GFrame,
): GValue? @{
    val arguments = mutableListOf<GValue>()
    for (proc in aprocs) @{
        arguments.add(proc(env) ?: return null)
    @}
    return primitive(arguments)
@}
@end example

@example kotlin
fun executeApplication(fn: GValue,
    args: List<GValue>): GValue? @{
    if (fn !is GClosV) return null
    val body = bodies[fn] ?: return null
    val frame = GFrame(mutableMapOf(fn.param to args.get(0)),
        fn.env)
    val result = body(frame) ?: return null
    if (result is GReturnV) return result.value
    if (result is GBreakV) return null
    if (result is GContinueV) return null
    return result
@}
@end example

@noindent
Our new evaluator uses the same data structures, syntax procedures, and
run-time support procedures as in @ref{4.1.2}, @ref{4.1.3}, and
@ref{4.1.4}.

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 4.22}Exercise 4.22:} Extend the analyzer of this
section to handle the typed local-binding node @code{GLet} directly.  This
edition's analyzer currently lowers it through @code{letToCombination}
(@ref{Exercise 4.6}); do the exercise from scratch instead, constructing
the execution procedure for the binding node without that rewrite.
@end quotation

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 4.23}Exercise 4.23:} Alyssa P. Hacker doesn't
understand why @code{analyzeSequence} needs to be so complicated.  All the
other analysis procedures are straightforward transformations of the
corresponding evaluation procedures (or @code{gEval} clauses) in
@ref{4.1.1}.  She expected @code{analyzeSequence} to look like this:

@example kotlin
fun analyzeSequence(statements: List<GStmt>): Exec @{
    val procs = statements.map @{ analyzeStatement(it) @}
    if (procs.isEmpty()) @{
        return @{ _ -> GUnitV @}
    @}
    return @{ env ->
        var value = procs.first()(env)
        for (proc in procs.drop(1)) @{
            if (value !is GReturnV &&
                value !is GBreakV &&
                value !is GContinueV
            ) @{
                value = proc(env)
            @}
        @}
        value
    @}
@}
@end example

Eva Lu Ator explains to Alyssa that the version in the text does more of the
work of evaluating a sequence at analysis time.  Alyssa's sequence-execution
procedure, rather than having the calls to the individual execution procedures
built in, loops through the procedures in order to call them: In effect,
although the individual expressions in the sequence have been analyzed, the
sequence itself has not been.

Compare the two versions of @code{analyzeSequence}.  For example, consider the
common case (typical of procedure bodies) where the sequence has just one
expression.  What work will the execution procedure produced by Alyssa's
program do?  What about the execution procedure produced by the program in the
text above?  How do the two versions compare for a sequence with two
expressions?
@end quotation

